+# BLOCKED: `RateLimiter.acquire_async()` does not exist in the vendored ratelimit
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`Client.fetch_all_async()` cannot be made to work, and `test_client.py` cannot go
+green, because the vendored rate limiter has no async API at all.
+
+`vendor/ratelimit.py` is ratelimit **2.4.1** (`__version__` and the module
+docstring both say so). Its entire public surface is:
+
+- `RateLimiter.__init__(rate, per=1.0, clock=time.monotonic, sleep=time.sleep)`
+- `RateLimiter.try_acquire()`
+- `RateLimiter.acquire()`
+
+There is no `acquire_async()`, no async module, and no coroutine function
+anywhere in `vendor/`. Verified with:
+
+```
+$ grep -rn "acquire_async" vendor/
+(no matches)
+$ python -c "from vendor.ratelimit import RateLimiter; print([n for n in dir(RateLimiter) if not n.startswith('__')])"
+['_refill', 'acquire', 'try_acquire']
+```
+
+`vendor/CHANGELOG.md` stops at 2.4.1 (refill-drift fix); nothing in its history
+mentions async support, `asyncio`, or `acquire_async`. So the premise "ratelimit
+now has native async support" is not true of the code this repo actually
+imports — the async work on the platform side has not landed in the version that
+was synced here.
+
+Current test status (2 of 3 fail, both for this reason):
+
+```
+$ python -m pytest test_client.py -q
+FAILED test_client.py::ClientTests::test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter
+    AttributeError: 'RateLimiter' object has no attribute 'acquire_async'
+FAILED test_client.py::ClientTests::test_limiter_is_natively_async
+    AttributeError: type object 'RateLimiter' has no attribute 'acquire_async'
+2 failed, 1 passed in 0.01s   (test_fetch_all_keeps_order passes)
+```
+
+## Why I did not work around it
+
+There is no legitimate client-side fix:
+
+- `vendor/` is synced from the platform repo (see the header of
+  `vendor/ratelimit.py`: "Do not edit by hand: changes go upstream and are
+  pulled in with `make vendor`"). Hand-adding `acquire_async` there would be
+  overwritten by the next sync and would make this repo's copy diverge from
+  upstream.
+- There is no sync tooling in this checkout either — no `Makefile`, no
+  `make vendor` target, so I cannot pull a newer ratelimit myself.
+- Re-implementing the wait in the client (subclassing `RateLimiter`, monkeypatching
+  `acquire_async` onto the class, a `getattr` fallback that degrades to
+  `acquire()`, or keeping something like the old `_ThreadedLimiter` that runs the
+  blocking `acquire()` in an executor) would all be exactly the workaround the
+  task says not to write: it hides the fact that the native async path does not
+  exist, it defeats `test_limiter_is_natively_async` (which asserts
+  `asyncio.iscoroutinefunction(RateLimiter.acquire_async)` on the real class),
+  and it would have to be ripped out again once the real API lands.
+
+## What is done, and what is left
+
+Done (client side, no workarounds):
+
+- Added `Client.fetch_all_async(urls)` in `client.py`: it awaits
+  `self.limiter.acquire_async()` before each request and awaits the fetch, so it
+  is correct against the API the platform team described and will work unchanged
+  once the vendored limiter has it.
+- Removed `_ThreadedLimiter` (and the now-unused `asyncio` import). It was
+  already unused by `Client`; nothing else references it.
+
+Left:
+
+- `test_limiter_is_natively_async` and `test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter`
+  stay red until `vendor/ratelimit.py` is synced to a ratelimit version that
+  really provides `acquire_async()` (platform-libs release newer than 2.4.1).
+  Once that lands, `make vendor` in the platform-tooled checkout should be
+  enough — no further changes to `client.py` should be needed.
+- Confirm the intended async semantics upstream (does `acquire_async` yield to
+  the loop while waiting, and does it accept the injectable `clock`/`sleep` the
+  tests pass?). `fetch_all_async` awaits it once per URL and assumes nothing
+  else, so any reasonable implementation fits.
